<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Okayama Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1871 CE · capital Okayama</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of Bizen, whose Confucian lord Ikeda Mitsumasa founded one of Japan's first schools for commoners and whose heirs built the Korakuen garden.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki">Kobayakawa Hideaki</a>, whose defection decided the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>, received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a> in 1600 but died without an heir in 1602. The Ikeda family then took over, and in 1632 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-mitsumasa">Ikeda Mitsumasa</a> moved there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori">Tottori</a>, exchanging domains with his cousin.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-mitsumasa">Mitsumasa</a>, a committed Confucian, employed the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumazawa-banzan">Kumazawa Banzan</a>, promoted flood control and land reclamation, restricted Buddhist temples and in 1670 founded the Shizutani school, open to commoners. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-tsunamasa">Ikeda Tsunamasa</a> completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koraku-en">Korakuen</a> garden in 1700. The domain, of about 315,000 koku, produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, salt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bizen-ware">Bizen ware</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a> joined the imperial side in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> of 1868. After abolition in 1871 it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama-prefecture">Okayama Prefecture</a>, and the Shizutani school building is now a national treasure.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/okayama-domain.jpg" alt="Okayama Domain" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Kimon Berlin · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Okayama_Castle.IMG_5917.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Okayama</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kobayakawa Hideaki, Ikeda Mitsumasa, Ikeda Tsunamasa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Confucianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bizen Domain</dd></dl></aside></div>
